1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Turning trash into clean burning power.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Neutralizing nuclear waste.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Capturing global warming gases and stuffing them back in the earth.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Meet the 21st century's growth industry.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
From green buildings to a river reborn.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, environmental tech on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The 21st century signals a crossroads for the planet known

8
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:43,000
as Earth and for its boldest, smartest, most insatiable

9
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:47,000
inhabitants, the human race.

10
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:51,000
Many of our accomplishments are double-edged.

11
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:55,000
Human beings have taken a staggering toll on the planet

12
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:57,000
that sustains us.

13
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:00,340
that sustains us.

14
00:01:00,341 --> 00:01:03,200
We've cleared 50% of the world's forests,

15
00:01:03,201 --> 00:01:07,460
eliminated countless species, dammed mighty rivers,

16
00:01:07,461 --> 00:01:10,820
and burned millions of years of stored fossil energy

17
00:01:10,821 --> 00:01:13,820
in a century and a half.

18
00:01:13,821 --> 00:01:15,760
Our greatest impact on the planet,

19
00:01:15,761 --> 00:01:18,460
and the greatest challenge for our civilization,

20
00:01:18,461 --> 00:01:21,000
will most likely be the steady climb in temperatures

21
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:21,960
over the next century.

22
00:01:21,961 --> 00:01:31,140
A consensus of national and international scientific organizations has linked the planet's current

23
00:01:31,141 --> 00:01:37,600
warming trend to human-created greenhouse gases, those gases that trap the sun's heat in our

24
00:01:37,601 --> 00:01:38,535
atmosphere.

25
00:01:38,536 --> 00:01:45,080
The most significant greenhouse gas is carbon dioxide, and the biggest source

26
00:01:45,081 --> 00:01:49,160
is our burning of oil, coal, and natural gas.

27
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:54,180
50 years from now, if we don't take major steps to reverse that trend,

28
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:57,860
we're going to have orders of magnitude more CO2 in the atmosphere

29
00:01:57,861 --> 00:02:01,360
than we've ever had in scientifically recorded history,

30
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:03,380
going back almost 700,000 years.

31
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:08,360
The consequences of that, you know, we know in general what they will be.

32
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:13,040
Floods, heat waves, shortages of water, shortages of food.

33
00:02:13,041 --> 00:02:15,100
We don't know what the scale will be.

34
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:21,580
One of the most important solutions to the carbon dioxide problem

35
00:02:21,581 --> 00:02:24,140
is unfolding at an unlikely place,

36
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:27,520
an oil field under the plains of Saskatchewan.

37
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:31,420
This oil reservoir, known as the Midale Field,

38
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:35,040
is also a proving ground for the 21st century technology

39
00:02:35,041 --> 00:02:37,820
known as geologic carbon sequestration,

40
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:42,440
capturing carbon emissions and burying them underground.

41
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:47,040
The Mydale Field, operated by Apache Canada Limited,

42
00:02:47,041 --> 00:02:51,040
was first drilled in 1953.

43
00:02:51,041 --> 00:02:56,040
It has a total capacity of 515 million barrels of oil.

44
00:02:56,041 --> 00:02:59,040
But as with many reservoirs in the world,

45
00:02:59,041 --> 00:03:03,040
the easily recoverable oil has already been extracted.

46
00:03:03,041 --> 00:03:06,040
The oil here at Mydale is contained in rock similar to what

47
00:03:06,041 --> 00:03:08,040
you see here.

48
00:03:08,041 --> 00:03:09,040
It is rock.

49
00:03:09,041 --> 00:03:10,040
It's very solid.

50
00:03:10,041 --> 00:03:13,540
solid, looks somewhat like a sponge full of what they call

51
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:17,180
bugs. Those bugs are filled with oil and in some cases oil and

52
00:03:17,181 --> 00:03:21,960
water. What is needed to be done is to get that oil from these

53
00:03:21,961 --> 00:03:27,220
rocks to a well to the surface. To do that you need pressure.

54
00:03:27,221 --> 00:03:33,700
Since the 1960s, water injection has been used at the

55
00:03:33,701 --> 00:03:37,360
Midale field to increase underground pressure and to push

56
00:03:37,361 --> 00:03:39,700
the oil toward the producing wells.

57
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:42,600
But a large amount of the oil will cling to the rocks,

58
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,100
and that cannot be stripped using water.

59
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:49,040
What needs to be done is a solvent has to be injected

60
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:53,280
down hole to combine in a miscible manner with the oil

61
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:56,080
to strip it from the rocks.

62
00:03:56,180 --> 00:04:01,180
Apache has found that solvent in the form of carbon dioxide.

63
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:03,420
The CO2 at this pipeline has been compressed

64
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:06,520
to about 2,000 pounds per square inch,

65
00:04:06,521 --> 00:04:08,180
at which point it behaves like a liquid.

66
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:15,100
It's then injected directly into the reservoir, 4,700 feet

67
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:17,200
below the ground.

68
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:19,840
When we inject CO2 into the reservoir,

69
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:22,640
it goes into solution with the oil,

70
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:25,900
changes the chemical and physical properties of the oil,

71
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,040
allows it to break the bond it has with the rocks,

72
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:32,580
and therefore we can produce that oil that we wouldn't otherwise

73
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:35,840
be able to do.

74
00:04:35,841 --> 00:04:41,100
Using CO2 to free oil from rock isn't a new technology.

75
00:04:41,101 --> 00:04:43,240
What's new is where the CO2 is coming from.

76
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:50,000
200 miles to the south, in Beulah, North Dakota,

77
00:04:50,001 --> 00:04:52,660
is the Dakota Gasification Company, which

78
00:04:52,661 --> 00:04:56,760
burns coal to make synthetic fuels.

79
00:04:56,761 --> 00:05:00,700
The process also emits a lot of carbon dioxide into the air,

80
00:05:00,701 --> 00:05:04,180
or it did until recently.

81
00:05:04,181 --> 00:05:07,760
Now the CO2 emissions are captured at the smokestacks,

82
00:05:07,761 --> 00:05:12,280
compressed, and sent to a 200-mile pipeline into Canada.

83
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:18,640
Both the Midale field and the adjacent Weyburn field,

84
00:05:18,641 --> 00:05:22,340
operated by Encana, use the CO2 in what's

85
00:05:22,341 --> 00:05:25,860
called Enhanced Oil Recovery, or EOR.

86
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:32,080
After the CO2 has loosened the oil from the rock,

87
00:05:32,081 --> 00:05:35,500
the injector shoots down a water flood, which sweeps the reservoir.

88
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,480
That is how we can direct the fluids in the reservoir

89
00:05:38,481 --> 00:05:42,380
from where we inject them to where we produce them.

90
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:47,360
This well behind us is producing about 650 barrels a day of fluid,

91
00:05:47,361 --> 00:05:51,740
running at about 85% water, 15% oil.

92
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:57,780
The witch's brew of oil, water, natural gas, and CO2

93
00:05:57,781 --> 00:06:00,100
is separated into its various parts.

94
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:05,420
Some of the CO2 will remain underground, tightly bound

95
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:06,920
to the rock.

96
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:10,560
The rest will be repressurized to 2,000 PSI,

97
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:13,160
combined with the Dakota pipeline CO2,

98
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:15,020
and injected into the ground once again.

99
00:06:17,860 --> 00:06:20,200
Over the next 40 years, the Dakota CO2

100
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:23,840
will continue to strip off all the oil it can.

101
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:27,240
As it does so, it will fill the void in the rock left

102
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:28,840
by the oil.

103
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:31,380
Under the water flood plan, we anticipated

104
00:06:31,381 --> 00:06:35,380
that the life of the field was about 15 years.

105
00:06:35,381 --> 00:06:39,880
With CO2, we anticipate that the field will still be functional

106
00:06:39,881 --> 00:06:42,380
in 2045.

107
00:06:42,381 --> 00:06:46,380
The greenhouse gas will then be buried in the oil reservoir

108
00:06:46,381 --> 00:06:50,380
and all 280 wells capped in cement.

109
00:06:50,381 --> 00:06:53,380
The same dense rock that trapped oil and natural gas for millions

110
00:06:53,381 --> 00:06:57,380
of years should trap the CO2 for just as long.

111
00:06:57,381 --> 00:07:04,380
Besides oil reservoirs, scientists have targeted other areas for carbon sequestration,

112
00:07:04,381 --> 00:07:09,380
most notably deep saline aquifers and abandoned coal beds.

113
00:07:09,381 --> 00:07:18,380
The global estimate for carbon dioxide emissions currently is between 23 and 24 gigatons of

114
00:07:18,381 --> 00:07:20,035
carbon dioxide per year.

115
00:07:20,036 --> 00:07:24,380
The estimates of the global potential for geological storage is

116
00:07:24,381 --> 00:07:26,880
is around 900 gigatons.

117
00:07:26,881 --> 00:07:29,620
There's more than enough potential for geological storage

118
00:07:29,621 --> 00:07:33,620
to look after our global emissions at this point.

119
00:07:33,621 --> 00:07:36,620
But carbon sequestration is costly.

120
00:07:36,621 --> 00:07:40,620
The price tag for the Midale and Weyburn CO2 injection projects

121
00:07:40,621 --> 00:07:44,540
is about $200 million, not including the CO2 itself.

122
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:53,680
But this hefty investment will allow Apache and Encana

123
00:07:53,681 --> 00:07:57,980
to generate 215 million barrels of otherwise unreachable oil,

124
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,020
worth billions of dollars, an obvious incentive.

125
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:05,260
The question is, who will pay for CO2 sequestration

126
00:08:05,261 --> 00:08:06,980
when there's no new oil to be recovered?

127
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:11,960
The drive to find ways to reduce or store carbon

128
00:08:11,961 --> 00:08:14,740
so that it doesn't get emitted into the atmosphere

129
00:08:14,741 --> 00:08:19,300
is increasingly coming into the regulatory arena

130
00:08:19,301 --> 00:08:23,040
with states like California putting limits

131
00:08:23,041 --> 00:08:25,280
on the amount of carbon that utilities can emit.

132
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:29,860
And in those situations, in order to keep on serving their customers

133
00:08:29,861 --> 00:08:33,720
and providing electricity, the utilities are going to have to find ways

134
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:36,480
to capture and store carbon,

135
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:40,260
or they're going to have to abandon technologies

136
00:08:40,261 --> 00:08:42,560
that have worked very well for them in the past.

137
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:47,300
Far from the windswept plains of Saskatchewan,

138
00:08:47,301 --> 00:08:50,180
in the sun-baked desert of Arizona,

139
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:54,540
Another company is finding a useful way to dispose of its carbon emissions.

140
00:08:55,140 --> 00:08:57,540
And it's quite literally green.

141
00:08:59,380 --> 00:09:05,180
The Red Hawk Power Generating Station, owned by Arizona Public Service, or APS,

142
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:08,840
is a large combined-cycle natural gas power plant.

143
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:16,220
It burns natural gas to turn giant turbines, which generate 1,000 megawatts of electricity.

144
00:09:16,221 --> 00:09:25,920
The major waste products from burning hydrocarbons are water and carbon dioxide, which is normally emitted as a greenhouse gas.

145
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:35,260
But combine H2O, CO2, lots of Arizona sunshine, and you also get the magic formula for photosynthesis.

146
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:42,580
So APS teamed up with a company called Green Fuel Technologies in this bold experiment.

147
00:09:42,581 --> 00:09:47,840
capturing a portion of the power plant's greenhouse gases with algae.

148
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:53,620
Algae has essentially one mission in life, eat CO2 and divide,

149
00:09:54,260 --> 00:10:00,160
and that makes it absolutely ideal to handle our CO2 greenhouse emissions issues.

150
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:05,880
The CO2 and wastewater are captured from the natural gas-burning flue

151
00:10:05,881 --> 00:10:08,720
and piped over to these bioreactor tanks.

152
00:10:08,721 --> 00:10:14,060
Like all plants, the algae consume the carbon as food for their growth process

153
00:10:14,061 --> 00:10:16,420
and release only oxygen.

154
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:21,140
The process begins in the nursery,

155
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:24,980
where green fuel biologists raise various strains of algae.

156
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:31,060
Almost all species of algae will double in one photo period of a day for eight hours.

157
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:33,940
And in here, we get three photo periods in the nursery.

158
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:37,620
We use the wastewater from the plant to grow these algae.

159
00:10:37,621 --> 00:10:44,540
So we adapt these algae to this particular location, to this particular water, to this particular flue gas.

160
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:47,800
That makes them very robust relative to this area.

161
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:53,340
The Wonder Plant is then transferred to the bioreactor,

162
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:58,820
which is designed to evenly distribute sunlight and CO2 until the algae are ready to harvest.

163
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:03,160
This is a line that allows us to sparge the gas into the system.

164
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:06,720
These lines here are for the cooling loops that we have.

165
00:11:06,721 --> 00:11:08,660
and this is a harvest loop.

166
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:13,180
When you finish growing the algae up to a particular concentration,

167
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:15,280
you can drain some of it off.

168
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:19,900
APS and green fuel aren't simply going to throw the algae away.

169
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:24,780
If the harvested algae sludge looks a lot like oil, it's no coincidence.

170
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:30,680
Like hydrocarbons, algae are chemically complex and bursting with energy.

171
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:35,180
There's a three-step process that we're looking at right now for using the algae.

172
00:11:35,181 --> 00:11:39,180
The first step, we harvest the oils from the algae to make biodiesel.

173
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:44,940
In the second step, we take the starch that's in the algae and we make ethanol from that.

174
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:50,040
And what's left of the algae are proteins, and we can use that for cattle feed.

175
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:55,300
The harvested algae can also be dried and burned again in the power plant.

176
00:11:55,540 --> 00:11:59,220
But you can also make paper from it. You can make plastics from it.

177
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:01,240
You can make food from it.

178
00:12:01,241 --> 00:12:06,380
In fact, most of us consume a lot of algae in our food every day without even realizing it.

179
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:16,120
I suspect over time we'll develop a closer symbiotic relationship with this plant that's been around for millions and millions of years,

180
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:17,880
certainly long before we were here.

181
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:26,860
The experiment has been so successful that APS and Green Fuel will soon be filling this entire structure with algae troughs.

182
00:12:26,861 --> 00:12:32,920
Scaling up this technology further will involve a much bigger leap.

183
00:12:34,800 --> 00:12:40,340
Capturing 100% of a plant's CO2 emissions will require about 2 acres of algae per megawatt,

184
00:12:41,260 --> 00:12:44,920
meaning a large plant like Red Hawk will need 2,000 acres.

185
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:49,340
But don't think Green Fuel Technologies hasn't thought of that.

186
00:12:49,341 --> 00:12:54,020
In the United States, there are over 1,700 large power facilities

187
00:12:54,021 --> 00:12:57,300
that are large enough to site facilities like this.

188
00:12:58,260 --> 00:13:00,980
And with potentially profitable byproducts,

189
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:04,780
algae's role in reducing carbon emissions may pay for itself.

190
00:13:08,280 --> 00:13:12,260
Reversing the carbon problem will require a number of simultaneous solutions,

191
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:16,520
from saving forests and wetlands that naturally capture CO2

192
00:13:16,521 --> 00:13:19,480
to expanding renewable energy technologies

193
00:13:19,481 --> 00:13:22,140
that never release carbon in the first place.

194
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:28,020
Another solution to serious environmental crises is rising

195
00:13:28,021 --> 00:13:29,020
from the ground up.

196
00:13:29,021 --> 00:13:34,020
The green home and the green workplace.

197
00:13:34,021 --> 00:13:38,020
Over the course of the project, the Meydale and Weyburn oil fields

198
00:13:38,021 --> 00:13:42,020
will sequester about 43 million tons of carbon dioxide,

199
00:13:42,021 --> 00:13:47,020
the equivalent of removing 8.8 million cars from the road for a year.

200
00:13:49,020 --> 00:13:52,020
To find a root cause of many environmental woes,

201
00:13:52,021 --> 00:13:58,780
woes look no further than your own home or your own office. Buildings put an immense

202
00:13:58,781 --> 00:14:01,032
strain on the planet's resources.

203
00:14:01,033 --> 00:14:04,580
Probably 50, 60 percent of the power in this country

204
00:14:04,581 --> 00:14:11,060
goes not to cars, which you hear a lot about, but rather to air-conditioned heat and light

205
00:14:11,061 --> 00:14:16,000
buildings. Enter the Green Building. Its three-fold goal?

206
00:14:16,001 --> 00:14:18,600
Use fewer and more sustainable

207
00:14:18,601 --> 00:14:26,200
building materials, dramatically reduce energy and water consumption, and build without the

208
00:14:26,201 --> 00:14:28,091
use of toxic chemicals.

209
00:14:28,092 --> 00:14:34,340
Getting those technologies deployed as widely as they could be is by far the

210
00:14:34,341 --> 00:14:43,020
cheapest, fastest area for looking at both reducing greenhouse gas emissions and reducing overall the

211
00:14:43,021 --> 00:14:48,360
emissions of conventional air pollutants.

212
00:14:48,361 --> 00:14:51,000
Living Proof is this sleek, modernist, three-bedroom

213
00:14:51,001 --> 00:14:56,060
house built by Living Homes in Santa Monica, California.

214
00:14:56,061 --> 00:15:01,380
Environmental considerations began even before construction.

215
00:15:01,381 --> 00:15:04,640
The Living Home consists of 11 prefabricated steel-framed

216
00:15:04,641 --> 00:15:10,540
modules, which were assembled on site in just one day.

217
00:15:10,541 --> 00:15:12,480
The prefab design greatly cuts down

218
00:15:12,481 --> 00:15:16,720
construction waste. Standard home construction is extremely

219
00:15:16,721 --> 00:15:20,320
wasteful. 30-40% of your materials will end up in landfill.

220
00:15:20,321 --> 00:15:24,160
And indeed, if you talk to the landfill guys, 30-40% of landfills

221
00:15:24,161 --> 00:15:28,800
are construction waste. When you prefabricate, in general, on

222
00:15:28,801 --> 00:15:31,760
average, about 2% of your materials end up in construction

223
00:15:31,761 --> 00:15:34,400
waste because you use shop drawings for everything. It's

224
00:15:34,401 --> 00:15:39,680
very precisely cut. The steel frame is also free of the

225
00:15:39,681 --> 00:15:42,080
mold and moisture issues common to wood.

226
00:15:42,081 --> 00:15:43,680
It's a very healthy material.

227
00:15:43,681 --> 00:15:45,680
It's also extremely recycled.

228
00:15:45,681 --> 00:15:52,640
Recycled and sustainable materials abound, and all surfaces were built without toxic

229
00:15:52,641 --> 00:15:57,680
chemicals, such as formaldehyde, which is commonly used in adhesives and coatings.

230
00:15:57,681 --> 00:16:01,700
The counters in the kitchen are made out of newsprint or cellulose.

231
00:16:01,701 --> 00:16:06,420
In the bathrooms, we have counters that are made out of recycled glass, shower dividers

232
00:16:06,421 --> 00:16:08,900
that are made out of reclaimed plastic resin.

233
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:12,620
Where wood is used,

234
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,760
it's cut from a sustainably harvested stand

235
00:16:14,761 --> 00:16:17,320
as certified by the Forest Stewardship Council.

236
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:20,740
This practice helps to preserve

237
00:16:20,741 --> 00:16:23,160
the world's best source of carbon sequestration,

238
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:25,240
old-growth forests.

239
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:28,380
The cost of building this home,

240
00:16:28,540 --> 00:16:30,560
designed by renowned architect Ray Cappy,

241
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:34,000
is $250 per square foot,

242
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:36,220
above the national average.

243
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:42,880
But the costs of building green can gradually be offset.

244
00:16:42,881 --> 00:16:48,900
Ideally, a green home generates much of its own energy, and then uses that energy as efficiently

245
00:16:48,901 --> 00:16:50,900
as possible.

246
00:16:50,901 --> 00:16:57,780
A solar thermal system heats all the hot water, which is also piped under the floor to provide

247
00:16:57,781 --> 00:17:01,660
efficient radiant heat from the ground up.

248
00:17:01,661 --> 00:17:05,640
Photovoltaic panels provide nearly all of the building's electricity.

249
00:17:05,641 --> 00:17:08,640
a feat made easier by highly efficient Energy Star appliances,

250
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:13,600
LED lights which use one-tenth the power of incandescence,

251
00:17:14,820 --> 00:17:17,380
and the use of free, abundant sunshine.

252
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:22,860
Green living isn't just for sunny California homes.

253
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:26,280
Across the nation, in the heart of New York City,

254
00:17:26,760 --> 00:17:31,060
the same concepts are being put into action on a much larger scale.

255
00:17:31,061 --> 00:17:38,020
This is the Solaire, a high-rise residential tower, home to nearly 700 residents in Lower

256
00:17:38,021 --> 00:17:40,120
Manhattan.

257
00:17:40,121 --> 00:17:47,340
With its own water treatment system and efficient use of electricity, it uses 55% less water

258
00:17:47,341 --> 00:17:53,960
and 37% less energy than a comparable building.

259
00:17:53,961 --> 00:17:58,700
The Solaire's most impressive feature may be its two green roofs, which use the power

260
00:17:58,701 --> 00:18:03,400
of plants to insulate the building and capture rainwater.

261
00:18:03,401 --> 00:18:08,460
The main benefit here with the roof is it helps with the heat island effect.

262
00:18:08,461 --> 00:18:14,360
The heat island effect is created by a city's dark asphalt streets, and especially by thousands

263
00:18:14,361 --> 00:18:20,440
of dark absorptive roofs, which trap the sun's heat and make a city up to 6 degrees warmer

264
00:18:20,441 --> 00:18:24,920
than nearby areas during the summer.

265
00:18:24,921 --> 00:18:31,500
The plants reflect sunlight and cool the surrounding air through a process called evapotranspiration,

266
00:18:31,501 --> 00:18:32,980
the plant's way of sweating.

267
00:18:32,981 --> 00:18:35,080
The green roof is an insulator.

268
00:18:35,081 --> 00:18:39,800
It keeps the heat in the winter and out in the summertime.

269
00:18:39,801 --> 00:18:44,200
The plants are merely the top layer of the roof's sophisticated drainage system.

270
00:18:44,201 --> 00:18:49,300
During storms, the multi-layered green roof captures up to 80% of the rainwater, which

271
00:18:49,301 --> 00:18:53,360
normally flows into sewers that quickly overflow.

272
00:18:53,361 --> 00:18:56,300
Water runoff is the biggest source of coastal water pollution.

273
00:18:56,860 --> 00:19:01,560
The water tends to run off of buildings, into the gutter, and then out into the ocean.

274
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:05,680
When it's in the gutter, it's picking up all of the filth, all of the animal feces,

275
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:10,880
all of the oil and toxic releases, carrying them out into the coastal waters.

276
00:19:11,620 --> 00:19:16,480
Living roofs are just one of the green technologies spreading in concrete jungles like New York.

277
00:19:16,481 --> 00:19:23,480
A few miles uptown from the Solaire, the new headquarters

278
00:19:23,481 --> 00:19:26,480
for the Hearst Corporation, one of this network's parent

279
00:19:26,481 --> 00:19:30,480
companies, proved that even a skyscraper can be green,

280
00:19:30,481 --> 00:19:33,480
from the ground up.

281
00:19:33,481 --> 00:19:38,480
For the 46-story Hearst Tower, this meant not just saving the

282
00:19:38,481 --> 00:19:43,480
Art Deco facade of the original headquarters, but recycling 85%

283
00:19:43,481 --> 00:19:45,780
85% of the old building's materials.

284
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:51,400
There was green thinking behind the unique steel exoskeleton, too.

285
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:55,880
By putting the skeleton of the building around on the outside

286
00:19:55,881 --> 00:19:57,800
so that you can actually see it,

287
00:19:58,460 --> 00:20:00,480
it became a much more efficient structure.

288
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:05,360
That triangle or diagrin uses less steel than a normal structure.

289
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:09,660
There are less columns throwing in the middle of your floor plate,

290
00:20:09,661 --> 00:20:13,400
but at the same time, it braces the building in a much more efficient way.

291
00:20:13,480 --> 00:20:20,480
More efficient framing means more opportunity for windows and natural light.

292
00:20:20,481 --> 00:20:26,480
Environmentally responsible design reached into major structural features and the smallest details.

293
00:20:26,481 --> 00:20:29,480
The ceiling itself is all made of recycled material.

294
00:20:29,481 --> 00:20:38,480
And the lighting systems that are built into the ceiling are programmable in the sense that when you walk out of the room they turn themselves off.

295
00:20:38,481 --> 00:20:42,480
They also adjust to all the natural light that comes in from outside.

296
00:20:42,481 --> 00:20:47,380
This wall system itself uses high-efficiency glass.

297
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:49,440
That means there's more iron in the glass,

298
00:20:49,680 --> 00:20:51,700
which allows more natural daylight in,

299
00:20:51,920 --> 00:20:54,740
but not enough that it actually gets too hot here,

300
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:57,540
which means that you need less artificial light.

301
00:20:59,300 --> 00:21:02,720
Early skyscrapers were in some ways green pioneers.

302
00:21:04,060 --> 00:21:05,860
Built before the advent of air conditioning,

303
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:08,620
these buildings relied on deep-set windows

304
00:21:08,621 --> 00:21:10,680
to shade the interior from direct sun.

305
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:19,480
Narrow floor plates and windows that actually opened were used to create cross ventilation.

306
00:21:19,481 --> 00:21:28,480
Early tall buildings relied on the thermal mass of masonry to absorb heat during the day and release it at night.

307
00:21:28,481 --> 00:21:37,480
These forms of passive cooling have come full circle and are recognized today as green building practices.

308
00:21:37,481 --> 00:21:41,640
The Hearst Tower, like the Solaire, collects and recycles its own stormwater.

309
00:21:43,220 --> 00:21:45,120
It's just a lot more water.

310
00:21:45,121 --> 00:21:47,540
This tank is 14,000 gallons.

311
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:50,440
This is where the stormwater is held.

312
00:21:50,960 --> 00:21:53,820
The walls are 18-inch thick concrete walls.

313
00:21:55,240 --> 00:21:59,040
The stormwater is triple filtered and then pumped into the air conditioning system,

314
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:01,120
where it supplies the cooling towers.

315
00:22:01,620 --> 00:22:04,740
They're running 4,000 tons of cooling through those towers,

316
00:22:05,260 --> 00:22:06,740
of course in the height of the summer.

317
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:09,240
That's your biggest usage.

318
00:22:09,340 --> 00:22:12,720
That's what's driving your water meter.

319
00:22:12,820 --> 00:22:14,480
The storm runoff also supplies

320
00:22:14,580 --> 00:22:18,020
the distinctive water feature known as ice fall, which

321
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:22,020
cools and humidifies the atrium.

322
00:22:22,120 --> 00:22:24,440
Thanks largely to the stormwater system,

323
00:22:24,540 --> 00:22:26,840
the Hearst Tower uses 10% less water

324
00:22:26,940 --> 00:22:29,840
than a normal skyscraper.

325
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:33,740
It also uses 26% less electricity.

326
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:36,340
That will lead to another kind of green saving.

327
00:22:36,341 --> 00:22:43,780
This is our home for the next hundred years, and those technologies, if you take 26% energy savings just in the electric alone,

328
00:22:44,120 --> 00:22:50,320
and then extrapolate that over the lifetime of this building, it's truly efficient how much money we're going to save as a corporation.

329
00:22:51,220 --> 00:22:55,580
There are plenty of people that thought you couldn't build green in New York City, and I think we proved them wrong.

330
00:22:55,780 --> 00:23:02,400
And the advice that I would give anyone that would undertake this project is come look at our project, do what we did, and then do one better.

331
00:23:02,401 --> 00:23:06,400
The green building revolution is something everyone can

332
00:23:06,401 --> 00:23:09,400
participate in.

333
00:23:09,401 --> 00:23:13,400
Other environmental technologies are best left to the experts,

334
00:23:13,401 --> 00:23:18,400
like cleaning up a deadly legacy of the nuclear age.

335
00:23:18,401 --> 00:23:21,400
If every American household replaced just one incandescent

336
00:23:21,401 --> 00:23:24,400
light bulb with a compact fluorescent bulb,

337
00:23:24,401 --> 00:23:27,400
the amount of pollution avoided would be equivalent to

338
00:23:27,401 --> 00:23:31,400
removing nearly 800,000 cars from the road.

339
00:23:32,400 --> 00:23:39,400
This is the Department of Energy's top secret Y-12 complex in Oak Ridge, Tennessee.

340
00:23:39,401 --> 00:23:45,400
Oak Ridge National Lab was one of the United States' principal sites for enriching uranium

341
00:23:45,401 --> 00:23:50,400
and developing nuclear bombs. From World War II to the end of the Cold War,

342
00:23:50,401 --> 00:23:57,400
much of the work took place at Y-12. There's actually three plants, Y-12, X-10,

343
00:23:57,401 --> 00:23:59,800
X-10 and K-25.

344
00:24:00,640 --> 00:24:03,920
And it was all part of the Manhattan Project during World War II

345
00:24:03,921 --> 00:24:09,360
to help develop nuclear weapons and get the first uranium enriched

346
00:24:09,361 --> 00:24:14,680
in order to develop the bombs that were used in Hiroshima, for example.

347
00:24:16,440 --> 00:24:21,200
Over the decades, the uranium and other toxic wastes were simply buried underground.

348
00:24:21,540 --> 00:24:25,480
They were doing this all very rapidly, so they didn't really have time to get rid of the waste

349
00:24:25,481 --> 00:24:27,840
in what we would consider now a proper fashion.

350
00:24:28,260 --> 00:24:31,700
So wastes were pumped from the Y-12 plant

351
00:24:31,701 --> 00:24:35,940
where they were processing parts for nuclear weapons

352
00:24:35,941 --> 00:24:39,240
and discharging the waste in the ponds there.

353
00:24:40,820 --> 00:24:43,820
Y-12 discharged millions of gallons of waste

354
00:24:43,821 --> 00:24:47,500
from nuclear weapons manufacturing into these ponds until 1984.

355
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:52,460
The ponds were then drained, capped, and covered with a parking lot.

356
00:24:55,480 --> 00:25:03,360
But that didn't stop the highly mobile, water-soluble form of uranium, known as U6, from leaching into the local groundwater,

357
00:25:03,740 --> 00:25:07,560
which contains up to 1,000 times the legal concentration of uranium.

358
00:25:10,200 --> 00:25:17,840
The first step in cleaning up this mess is to take a sample of the groundwater layer, which is roughly 30% water mixed with soil.

359
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:22,340
When we bring up a core, we'll test it in various ways.

360
00:25:22,341 --> 00:25:25,580
We'll start by scanning it with a beta gamma meter,

361
00:25:25,581 --> 00:25:27,740
which is this instrument here.

362
00:25:27,741 --> 00:25:30,620
And this is a sample we've taken earlier from our field plot.

363
00:25:30,621 --> 00:25:33,860
It has a fair amount of activity in it.

364
00:25:33,861 --> 00:25:35,860
As you can hear, the counts go off.

365
00:25:35,861 --> 00:25:40,400
The more counts, the more activity.

366
00:25:40,401 --> 00:25:42,500
We can interrogate it further with something

367
00:25:42,501 --> 00:25:44,300
called the x-ray fluorimeter.

368
00:25:44,301 --> 00:25:46,160
And it sends out those x-rays.

369
00:25:46,161 --> 00:25:49,600
And then you can tell by how much each individual element

370
00:25:49,601 --> 00:25:52,340
fluoresces, how much of those elements are in here.

371
00:25:52,341 --> 00:25:55,760
and you can actually quantify it in the parts per million level.

372
00:25:57,520 --> 00:26:00,680
Y12 and other Department of Energy sites are facing a challenge

373
00:26:00,681 --> 00:26:03,000
that until recently was thought insurmountable.

374
00:26:03,560 --> 00:26:07,900
There's probably about 32 different states that have uranium contamination.

375
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:12,640
A lot of it's related to both the mining of the uranium

376
00:26:12,641 --> 00:26:15,420
and actually the processing of it at different plants.

377
00:26:15,421 --> 00:26:20,500
So it's fairly widespread, and it's the DOE problem in particular

378
00:26:20,501 --> 00:26:23,380
because of the business that DOE is in.

379
00:26:24,760 --> 00:26:29,120
To tackle what may end up being the largest environmental cleanup job in history,

380
00:26:30,200 --> 00:26:33,200
scientists from Oak Ridge National Lab have begun experimenting

381
00:26:33,201 --> 00:26:36,040
with an innovative form of bioremediation.

382
00:26:37,420 --> 00:26:42,860
Bioremediation is a methodology that uses living systems like microbes or bacteria

383
00:26:42,861 --> 00:26:47,960
to actually process the contaminants in the soil into forms that are less hazardous.

384
00:26:47,961 --> 00:26:52,960
Using bioremediation to stabilize heavy metals like uranium-6

385
00:26:52,961 --> 00:26:55,960
was a radical, unproven technology.

386
00:26:55,961 --> 00:27:00,960
A tough job became even tougher when the soil and groundwater at Y-12

387
00:27:00,961 --> 00:27:04,960
were revealed to contain so many toxins besides uranium

388
00:27:04,961 --> 00:27:08,960
that helpful microbes didn't stand a chance of survival.

389
00:27:08,961 --> 00:27:13,960
It has PCE in it, which is a toxic organic,

390
00:27:13,961 --> 00:27:17,960
and an array of different types of metals, nickel,

391
00:27:17,961 --> 00:27:20,460
very high concentrations of aluminum.

392
00:27:20,461 --> 00:27:23,960
The acidity is so strong, it's dissolving calcareous-type

393
00:27:23,961 --> 00:27:24,960
minerals in the ground.

394
00:27:24,961 --> 00:27:28,960
And the odor of nitric acid is very apparent.

395
00:27:28,961 --> 00:27:31,960
So before scientists can even attempt to bioremediate

396
00:27:31,961 --> 00:27:35,960
the uranium, they pump the groundwater to the surface

397
00:27:35,961 --> 00:27:39,960
and through a gauntlet of chemical transformations.

398
00:27:39,961 --> 00:27:42,960
The first uses a vacuum stripper and carbon filters

399
00:27:42,961 --> 00:27:48,460
carbon filters to remove volatile organic toxins from the water.

400
00:27:48,461 --> 00:27:53,400
The next step is the groundwater is brought over to these holding tanks where the first

401
00:27:53,401 --> 00:27:55,433
thing we have to remove is the aluminum.

402
00:27:55,434 --> 00:27:58,280
And at this particular site, the aluminum concentrations

403
00:27:58,281 --> 00:28:01,958
are very high on the order of like 400 to 500 parts per million.

404
00:28:01,959 --> 00:28:03,280
It's dredged and pumped

405
00:28:03,281 --> 00:28:06,700
into another holding tank in the back.

406
00:28:06,701 --> 00:28:11,460
The water still contains massive amounts of toxic nitrate, which was used to clean nuclear

407
00:28:11,461 --> 00:28:13,281
weapons instrumentation.

408
00:28:13,282 --> 00:28:19,200
So the water is pumped inside this fluidized bed reactor, where microorganisms

409
00:28:19,201 --> 00:28:20,986
denitrify the nitrate.

410
00:28:20,987 --> 00:28:26,180
Finally, the water is pumped back underground, and the main task

411
00:28:26,181 --> 00:28:33,040
can begin, stimulating microscopic bugs to convert highly mobile, soluble uranium-6 into

412
00:28:33,041 --> 00:28:35,493
stable, insoluble uranium-4.

413
00:28:35,494 --> 00:28:40,400
The first step is to pump into the soil and groundwater,

414
00:28:40,401 --> 00:28:43,500
energy source that will stimulate the microbes.

415
00:28:43,501 --> 00:28:45,740
In this case, it's ethanol.

416
00:28:45,741 --> 00:28:48,540
So when we put methanol or ethanol or acetate in the

417
00:28:48,541 --> 00:28:50,880
groundwater, we're feeding the bacteria.

418
00:28:50,881 --> 00:28:53,140
And when they're fed, then they have to respire.

419
00:28:53,141 --> 00:28:58,960
They have to use the metals to do that.

420
00:28:58,961 --> 00:29:02,920
As the bacteria respire, they release an extra electron,

421
00:29:02,921 --> 00:29:06,660
which attaches to certain metals, including uranium.

422
00:29:06,661 --> 00:29:09,360
Electrons, which carry a negative charge,

423
00:29:09,361 --> 00:29:12,600
reduce the oxidation state of uranium.

424
00:29:12,601 --> 00:29:15,060
That is, they lower the number of positive charges

425
00:29:15,061 --> 00:29:18,120
on each uranium atom.

426
00:29:18,121 --> 00:29:19,720
That's a very, very important feature,

427
00:29:19,721 --> 00:29:22,960
because if I reduce, for example, the oxidation state

428
00:29:22,961 --> 00:29:26,640
of uranium from uranium-6 to uranium-4,

429
00:29:26,641 --> 00:29:30,220
that uranium is no longer soluble in water.

430
00:29:30,221 --> 00:29:32,680
It doesn't flow along with the groundwater.

431
00:29:32,681 --> 00:29:35,340
It precipitates out and sits there in the soil

432
00:29:35,341 --> 00:29:38,360
in a very stable way.

433
00:29:38,361 --> 00:29:43,000
Thus far, the stable uranium-4 is sticking to the soil and away from the groundwater.

434
00:29:44,420 --> 00:29:49,020
The project's remarkable success has attracted worldwide scientific attention.

435
00:29:50,380 --> 00:29:52,820
Although still unproven on a large scale,

436
00:29:53,460 --> 00:29:58,480
it's seen as a major breakthrough in stabilizing not just uranium, but other toxic metals.

437
00:29:59,200 --> 00:30:05,920
For the DOE cleanup job alone, bioremediation may also save taxpayers an awfully steep bill.

438
00:30:05,921 --> 00:30:11,740
The estimated cost of cleaning that up using conventional technologies is something like $300 billion.

439
00:30:12,860 --> 00:30:18,860
The bioremediation approach can be much cheaper, actually safer, because you don't have to dig it up.

440
00:30:19,220 --> 00:30:22,640
You don't have to truck it over roads. You don't have to expose people to it.

441
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:30,740
As scientists at Oak Ridge National Laboratory continue to study this microscopic solution to a gigantic waste problem,

442
00:30:30,741 --> 00:30:35,740
Industry is finding novel uses for other sources of waste,

443
00:30:35,741 --> 00:30:40,740
from paper sludge to millions of tons of landfill garbage.

444
00:30:40,741 --> 00:30:46,740
The Department of Energy is faced with cleaning up 1.4 billion

445
00:30:46,741 --> 00:30:50,740
cubic feet of contaminated soil, enough to fill 17 sports

446
00:30:50,741 --> 00:30:57,740
stadiums and 1.7 trillion gallons of contaminated groundwater.

447
00:30:57,741 --> 00:30:59,900
Recycling.

448
00:31:00,380 --> 00:31:03,520
It's become almost a cliche of the environmental movement

449
00:31:03,521 --> 00:31:06,740
and as routine as taking out the garbage.

450
00:31:08,280 --> 00:31:12,220
But recycling waste materials into useful products can and does

451
00:31:12,221 --> 00:31:14,500
have profound impacts on the environment.

452
00:31:16,480 --> 00:31:18,140
Take the aluminum can.

453
00:31:18,680 --> 00:31:21,740
95% of the energy saved when you recycle aluminum,

454
00:31:22,420 --> 00:31:25,880
aluminum can, for example, versus making it in a virgin manner.

455
00:31:25,881 --> 00:31:32,700
Nationwide, it's like 15 million barrels of oil are saved through recycling aluminum cans and whatnot.

456
00:31:35,180 --> 00:31:42,160
One ton of recycled paper saves about 30 trees, not to mention 7,000 gallons of water,

457
00:31:43,340 --> 00:31:47,920
4,000 kilowatt hours of electricity, and 60 pounds of air pollutants.

458
00:31:47,921 --> 00:31:54,720
The paper industry, by design, is probably the second or third energy-intensive industry in the world.

459
00:31:55,880 --> 00:32:00,320
At Neenah Paper, located along the Fox River in Wisconsin,

460
00:32:00,321 --> 00:32:03,060
they're recycling a lot more than paper.

461
00:32:05,300 --> 00:32:08,480
Everything from water to sludge to steam power

462
00:32:08,481 --> 00:32:10,980
is part of a giant self-sustaining loop.

463
00:32:14,620 --> 00:32:16,900
As the papermaking process begins,

464
00:32:17,540 --> 00:32:19,420
Neenah Paper feeds significant amounts

465
00:32:19,421 --> 00:32:21,560
of post-consumer waste into its pulpers.

466
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:25,080
Post-consumer fiber, by its definition,

467
00:32:25,081 --> 00:32:27,780
is paper products, a lot of them heavily printed,

468
00:32:27,880 --> 00:32:30,960
that have met their final use from the end consumer.

469
00:32:31,060 --> 00:32:33,820
That company that collects it does some decoloring

470
00:32:33,920 --> 00:32:36,820
and then ships it in this final form, which is 50% moisture.

471
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:40,960
The recycled material is combined

472
00:32:41,060 --> 00:32:43,840
with virgin pulp, along with dyes, additives,

473
00:32:43,940 --> 00:32:44,940
and a lot more water.

474
00:32:47,800 --> 00:32:50,840
As the pulp begins its journey across the papermaker,

475
00:32:50,940 --> 00:32:53,140
it's composed of 90% water.

476
00:32:53,141 --> 00:33:00,940
The papermaking process is nothing more than a gigantic, controlled process of removing water from that solution.

477
00:33:02,840 --> 00:33:08,960
The papermaker applies gravity, pressure, and steam heat to remove this water.

478
00:33:10,500 --> 00:33:14,920
The extracted water and small bits of unused pulp then move to the treatment plant.

479
00:33:16,420 --> 00:33:20,360
30% of the water will be recycled back into the papermaking process.

480
00:33:20,361 --> 00:33:26,260
Thanks to extensive bioremediation, the remaining water is returned to the Fox River,

481
00:33:26,880 --> 00:33:28,100
cleaner than it left it.

482
00:33:30,200 --> 00:33:35,080
The solid waste, known in the paper industry by the technical term sludge,

483
00:33:35,360 --> 00:33:37,940
is too far gone to be recycled back into paper.

484
00:33:40,960 --> 00:33:47,040
So every day, 1,300 tons of the sludge from the Neenah Mill and other local paper mills

485
00:33:47,041 --> 00:33:50,120
are trucked to the nearby Fox Valley Energy Center.

486
00:33:50,360 --> 00:33:53,160
where the waste is burned cleanly as a biofuel.

487
00:33:53,580 --> 00:33:58,620
The solid portion of the sludge is about 50% paper fiber,

488
00:33:58,860 --> 00:34:01,880
which is good biomass material, and it burns very well.

489
00:34:02,260 --> 00:34:05,160
The remaining 50% of the solid material is inorganic.

490
00:34:05,500 --> 00:34:08,280
It's the ash, the mineral fiber, and clay,

491
00:34:08,380 --> 00:34:09,780
which does not burn very well.

492
00:34:10,660 --> 00:34:14,500
This inorganic material is melted into a glass-like substance,

493
00:34:14,800 --> 00:34:17,320
then flash-cooled into glass aggregate,

494
00:34:17,321 --> 00:34:21,440
which will be used in everything from asphalt to sandpaper.

495
00:34:22,020 --> 00:34:26,180
The organic portion of the sludge is burned in this seven-story boiler.

496
00:34:27,260 --> 00:34:31,700
This boiler is designed to produce 300,000 pounds per hour of steam

497
00:34:31,701 --> 00:34:35,780
at 350 pounds per square inch and 600 degrees F.

498
00:34:37,300 --> 00:34:41,500
This so-called green steam is then pipelined back to Neenah Paper,

499
00:34:42,020 --> 00:34:45,180
where it provides all the steam used to dry the paper.

500
00:34:47,320 --> 00:34:51,820
The green steam has almost entirely replaced the plant's natural gas boilers,

501
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:53,360
which often sit idle.

502
00:34:53,860 --> 00:34:58,660
We've reduced our consumption of natural gas by over 80 percent.

503
00:34:59,280 --> 00:35:04,140
So it replaces the fossil fuel with something that was a waste in the process before.

504
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:07,780
So it's great energy savings, great environmental savings.

505
00:35:08,580 --> 00:35:12,520
Turning the sludge into steam also brings benefits to the local environment.

506
00:35:12,521 --> 00:35:15,360
If this plant was shut down tomorrow,

507
00:35:15,460 --> 00:35:18,960
the local county landfills here would be full within a year.

508
00:35:21,900 --> 00:35:23,960
Landfills.

509
00:35:24,060 --> 00:35:27,960
Traditionally, they've been the antithesis of recycling,

510
00:35:28,060 --> 00:35:31,280
places where millions of tons of potentially useful material

511
00:35:31,380 --> 00:35:36,880
come to die a slow, unsightly, smelly death.

512
00:35:36,980 --> 00:35:40,380
They're also one of the world's leading producers of methane.

513
00:35:40,381 --> 00:35:44,380
This greenhouse gas, more damaging than carbon dioxide,

514
00:35:44,381 --> 00:35:48,380
is emitted as the garbage decomposes.

515
00:35:48,381 --> 00:35:52,380
But today's landfills have discovered how to recycle greenhouse gases

516
00:35:52,381 --> 00:35:56,380
into clean burning energy.

517
00:35:56,381 --> 00:36:00,380
At the South Shelby Landfill near Memphis, Tennessee,

518
00:36:00,381 --> 00:36:04,380
Allied Waste has teamed up with several environmental partners to harness the power of methane.

519
00:36:04,381 --> 00:36:08,380
What we're standing on top of is actually waste that's been buried

520
00:36:08,381 --> 00:36:11,380
It's been buried at least five years or more.

521
00:36:11,480 --> 00:36:14,280
And we're standing on top of an active live gas well

522
00:36:14,380 --> 00:36:16,880
that's extracting methane out from subsurface.

523
00:36:19,920 --> 00:36:24,720
120 of these perforated wells, sunk between 65 and 135

524
00:36:24,820 --> 00:36:28,060
feet into the garbage, pull up 5,000 cubic feet

525
00:36:28,160 --> 00:36:30,600
per minute of landfill gas.

526
00:36:30,700 --> 00:36:33,400
That's enough to heat 30,000 homes.

527
00:36:33,500 --> 00:36:37,780
These vertical wells are placed around 100 to 150 feet

528
00:36:37,781 --> 00:36:43,300
apart from each other, basically in a circle around the buried waste. What we

529
00:36:43,301 --> 00:36:48,040
want to do is create a zone of influence between these gas wells to where we're

530
00:36:48,041 --> 00:36:52,120
not having any kind of gas, any kind of methane migrate either outward or

531
00:36:52,121 --> 00:36:55,360
downward. We don't want to get into the groundwater, we don't want it to get into

532
00:36:55,361 --> 00:37:01,220
the atmosphere, and we don't want it to travel off-site somehow. A slight vacuum

533
00:37:01,221 --> 00:37:06,040
pulls all the gas to a common header. This is an 18-inch pipe. Everything comes

534
00:37:06,041 --> 00:37:07,540
It's extracted from the blower.

535
00:37:07,640 --> 00:37:10,340
The blower is pulling a gradual vacuum on this.

536
00:37:13,380 --> 00:37:14,840
After the gas is compressed,

537
00:37:14,940 --> 00:37:17,780
it's sent through this pipeline to the Soleil Company,

538
00:37:17,880 --> 00:37:21,180
a nearby food processing plant.

539
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:23,800
The plant, which produces various food products from

540
00:37:23,900 --> 00:37:26,900
soybeans, is the largest of its kind in the world.

541
00:37:29,700 --> 00:37:33,700
Landfill gas provides virtually 100% of the prodigious steam

542
00:37:33,701 --> 00:37:38,100
the prodigious steam heat needed for Soleil's processing.

543
00:37:38,101 --> 00:37:41,840
The old natural gas pipeline goes largely unused.

544
00:37:41,841 --> 00:37:45,840
The landfill gas does release carbon dioxide when burned,

545
00:37:45,841 --> 00:37:49,240
but it still brings a two-fold green benefit,

546
00:37:49,241 --> 00:37:53,060
eliminating methane, the more potent greenhouse gas,

547
00:37:53,061 --> 00:37:55,760
and replacing the CO2 emissions of the natural gas

548
00:37:55,761 --> 00:37:59,600
that's no longer burned.

549
00:37:59,601 --> 00:38:05,360
human ingenuity can turn a nuisance into a valuable resource.

550
00:38:05,361 --> 00:38:14,360
It can also return a river to its natural state, which may save cities as well as ecosystems.

551
00:38:14,361 --> 00:38:20,520
A landfill in Florida will soon be using Plasma Arc technology to vaporize its garbage at 10,000

552
00:38:20,521 --> 00:38:22,240
degrees Fahrenheit.

553
00:38:22,241 --> 00:38:28,360
The 3,000 tons of trash burned daily will generate enough electricity to power 36,000

554
00:38:28,361 --> 00:38:31,500
homes.

555
00:38:31,501 --> 00:38:35,200
When Hurricane Katrina left much of New Orleans underwater,

556
00:38:35,201 --> 00:38:37,280
it was easy to trace the deadly flood surge

557
00:38:37,281 --> 00:38:38,820
to the failed system of levees.

558
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:44,400
But behind the failed levees lay another, less obvious,

559
00:38:44,401 --> 00:38:48,400
reason for the catastrophic flooding,

560
00:38:48,401 --> 00:38:53,800
the steady disappearance of Louisiana's marshy wetlands.

561
00:38:53,801 --> 00:38:55,640
The wetlands have disappeared largely

562
00:38:55,641 --> 00:38:58,700
because for many decades, the Lower Mississippi River

563
00:38:58,701 --> 00:39:00,700
has been narrowly channeled and cut off

564
00:39:00,701 --> 00:39:02,040
from its traditional floodplain.

565
00:39:05,080 --> 00:39:08,180
The river can no longer deposit its nutrient-rich silt

566
00:39:08,181 --> 00:39:10,780
to create wetlands and barrier islands,

567
00:39:10,781 --> 00:39:13,640
which vanish under the encroaching Gulf of Mexico

568
00:39:13,641 --> 00:39:18,360
at the rate of 34 square miles every year.

569
00:39:18,361 --> 00:39:22,820
Wetlands and the river ecosystems known as riparian corridors

570
00:39:22,821 --> 00:39:24,580
are nature's built-in flood control.

571
00:39:28,220 --> 00:39:31,580
Hydraulic engineers estimate that every mile of wetland

572
00:39:31,581 --> 00:39:36,660
or riparian forest absorbs one foot of a hurricane storm surge.

573
00:39:36,661 --> 00:39:43,460
They act like huge sponges, and they have a really large ability

574
00:39:43,461 --> 00:39:45,580
to attenuate flood flows.

575
00:39:45,581 --> 00:39:48,580
And so riparian filter strips and forests

576
00:39:48,581 --> 00:39:51,420
can slow down the water, they can spread the water out,

577
00:39:51,421 --> 00:39:53,560
They can absorb some of that flood energy,

578
00:39:53,920 --> 00:39:59,860
and they can allow for the downstream areas more time to move that water through.

579
00:40:02,080 --> 00:40:06,840
2,000 miles away from Louisiana, in California's Central Valley,

580
00:40:07,820 --> 00:40:10,980
restoring riparian corridors has taken on a similar urgency.

581
00:40:12,960 --> 00:40:17,260
95% of California's riparian ecosystems are gone.

582
00:40:17,261 --> 00:40:26,560
In areas like the Sacramento San Joaquin Delta, home to 500,000 people and some of the nation's most valuable farmland,

583
00:40:27,220 --> 00:40:31,820
the only protection against a catastrophic flood is an aging system of levees.

584
00:40:32,660 --> 00:40:43,340
The city of Sacramento is ranked as the next highest or next most probable metropolitan area at risk in the country.

585
00:40:43,341 --> 00:40:49,700
River Partners, a non-profit group, is working on the long-term solution,

586
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:53,780
restoring California's decimated riparian corridors.

587
00:40:54,880 --> 00:41:01,580
They replace former flood-prone farmland, like this 1,700-acre project along the San Joaquin River,

588
00:41:02,120 --> 00:41:07,440
with the area's original ecosystem, including 270,000 trees.

589
00:41:07,441 --> 00:41:10,160
The two main goals?

590
00:41:10,860 --> 00:41:13,460
Building a high-quality riparian habitat for wildlife

591
00:41:13,461 --> 00:41:16,080
and rebuilding nature's flood buffers.

592
00:41:17,320 --> 00:41:19,160
The planting is hardly random.

593
00:41:20,120 --> 00:41:23,140
It's based on a computer simulation of a flood through the area

594
00:41:23,141 --> 00:41:27,280
and the varying structures of the plants themselves.

595
00:41:28,180 --> 00:41:32,900
In an area where we know that the velocity of the floodwaters would be higher,

596
00:41:33,640 --> 00:41:35,540
we would not plant trees.

597
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:36,860
Trees are very rigid.

598
00:41:36,861 --> 00:41:38,020
they'll back the water up.

599
00:41:38,120 --> 00:41:41,060
So we plant more flexible plants, such as the rose

600
00:41:41,160 --> 00:41:43,500
and the blackberry, which are very flexible

601
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:45,600
with the high-speed water.

602
00:41:45,700 --> 00:41:48,480
So that's a very critical part of the design here,

603
00:41:48,580 --> 00:41:52,780
is how will the flood waters move?

604
00:41:52,880 --> 00:41:54,720
While earth movers scoop out the areas

605
00:41:54,820 --> 00:41:57,780
that will become wetlands, tractors dig furrows

606
00:41:57,880 --> 00:42:01,380
for a precise mosaic of native trees and grasses,

607
00:42:01,480 --> 00:42:05,720
from valley oaks to coyote brush.

608
00:42:05,721 --> 00:42:11,400
In three years, this empty field will look like this.

609
00:42:11,401 --> 00:42:12,160
Pretty amazing.

610
00:42:12,161 --> 00:42:13,560
You build it, and they'll find it.

611
00:42:13,561 --> 00:42:14,600
They come.

612
00:42:14,601 --> 00:42:16,740
So I mean, right now, there's not a whole lot of wildlife

613
00:42:16,741 --> 00:42:19,060
out here, but you come out in three years,

614
00:42:19,061 --> 00:42:22,800
and it'll probably be teeming wildlife.

615
00:42:22,801 --> 00:42:25,040
For the first three years, river partners

616
00:42:25,041 --> 00:42:27,500
will drip and flood irrigate the plantings,

617
00:42:27,501 --> 00:42:31,180
using water from the San Joaquin River.

618
00:42:31,181 --> 00:42:35,340
After three years, the forest is self-sufficient.

619
00:42:35,341 --> 00:42:39,580
Vegetation is strategically planted on the levees themselves to prevent erosion.

620
00:42:42,740 --> 00:42:49,880
This levee, which was breached in the flood of 1997, will stay breached, replaced by the new riparian forest.

621
00:42:50,140 --> 00:42:53,280
Some of the lands, such as this area, flooded periodically.

622
00:42:53,660 --> 00:43:00,000
And after the 97 floods, there's four major landowners in this area had said enough.

623
00:43:00,180 --> 00:43:03,280
You know, their houses were flooded out, their barns, their livestock.

624
00:43:03,281 --> 00:43:12,260
And so it was kind of an economic process to realize this land is best suited as floodplain and natural environments, natural habitat.

625
00:43:14,420 --> 00:43:18,280
Healthy river corridors will do more than attract wildlife and provide recreation.

626
00:43:21,980 --> 00:43:30,580
In the long run, they'll preserve the long-term viability of fisheries, farms, and cities.

627
00:43:30,581 --> 00:43:34,580
As with other environmental technologies,

628
00:43:34,581 --> 00:43:37,580
there's a paradox at work.

629
00:43:37,581 --> 00:43:41,580
Human ingenuity restoring nature's delicate balance,

630
00:43:41,581 --> 00:43:44,580
which has been altered by human intervention.

631
00:43:47,580 --> 00:43:51,580
But this paradox is necessary because the health of the planet

632
00:43:51,581 --> 00:43:55,580
and the tenuous fate of civilization are linked more

633
00:43:55,581 --> 00:43:57,580
closely now than ever.

